            <ArticleTitle>[A woman in her thirties with pain in her fingers and toes].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>2401-3</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.4045/tidsskr.12.0379</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Hand pain is a relatively common complaint, and careful anamnesis and clinical examination may reveal its aetiology. Multiple joint involvement suggests either osteoarthritis or inflammatory arthritis. In the returning traveller, a more exotic explanation might be the case. A 31 year old woman was referred to our outpatient clinic for evaluation due to peripheral joint arthralgia. The symptoms started six months earlier, on the same day she returned from a three-week holiday in India. There were no signs of inflammatory arthritis or osteoarthritis. Blood tests were normal. Chikungunya virus serology was positive. The patient received symptomatic treatment with nonsteroid anti-inflammatory drugs and improved over a period of months. We describe the first case of Chikungunya fever diagnosed in our hospital.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
